Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:06.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Please join me in welcoming Hang Zhao for his keynote, Organizing Physical AI Across Timescales.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.35,0:00:30.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thanks for the kind introduction.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.25,0:00:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,They remind me a lot of good memories in the old times.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.75,0:00:56.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Great.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.79,0:01:05.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today I want to talk about time for robots, specifically how robots' intelligence should be organized across many different timescales.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.13,0:01:16.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,As the introduction goes, my own research path in physical AI started from self-driving, robots on wheels.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.83,0:01:20.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,At Waymo, I published my first oral paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.63,0:01:22.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was seven years ago.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.79,0:01:24.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then I moved to robots with legs.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.73,0:01:32.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,We spent a few years teaching legged robots, humanoid robots, to run, to jump, and to conquer tough terrains.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.65,0:01:34.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now we are working on robots with arms.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.89,0:01:43.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because in the end, we want our robots to do real physical work for our humans.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.59,0:01:51.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yesterday, when I landed in Austin, I found that the Waymo and the Cybercabs are all over the streets.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.33,0:02:00.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,For someone who started in self-driving research, I have to say that felt really good.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:00.81,0:02:04.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Driving has made it to everyday life and into production.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.49,0:02:07.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Can humanoid robots also make it too?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.35,0:02:13.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today, I'll share one piece of my answer, and it has to do with time.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.25,0:02:25.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very recently, GPT-6 Astra came out, and within days, people are plugging the model into their own robots and found that it just worked.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.71,0:02:31.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,NVIDIA researchers did a careful study on the RoboLab 120 simulation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.41,0:02:40.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,They found that GPT-6 Astra scored 66% of the tasks, as opposed to π0.5 on only 28%.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:42.87,0:02:47.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,On another simulation benchmark, RoboDojo, we got similar results.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.69,0:02:55.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,GPT-6 Astra scored 22, while other robot foundation models like π0.5 only scored 7.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.57,0:03:03.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is really surprising, and I believe many of us in this community are not ready for this.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.72,0:03:05.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,But there's a catch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.34,0:03:08.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,These benchmarks don't measure time.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.76,0:03:12.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,These are frames from one of the robot lab runs.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.68,0:03:14.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,The Astra has a direct policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:14.94,0:03:16.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,It controls the robot directly.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:17.14,0:03:23.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you look closely for each call of the foundation model it takes 18 seconds
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.81,0:03:29.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,and the robots spends 97 % of the time waiting for the model to think.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.97,0:03:34.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in reality a real robot cannot wait for the model to think.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.65,0:03:40.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then the question is, the frontier model is a very good thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.94,0:03:44.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,How can we actually make good use of them to solve the robotic tasks?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.95,0:03:54.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,To figure this out, I conducted a small controlled experiment in RoboCasa, which are simulators of home kitchens.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.44,0:03:56.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,And there are five test kitchens.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.37,0:04:03.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with five tasks, the kitchens are the ones the robot has never seen before.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.79,0:04:08.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it has 50 seconds of wall time for each task to finish.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:11.77,0:04:14.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how should the frontier model control the robot?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.17,0:04:16.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,I tried five ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.05,0:04:19.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first one is called coding policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.61,0:04:26.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Basically, we ask our frontier model to understand the task and write a piece of code.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.39,0:04:32.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,And at deployment time, the code in the program just runs without calling the foundation model.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.61,0:04:37.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the second method is called coding plus ask policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.99,0:04:42.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Basically, when the coding at deployment time, the code runs.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.11,0:04:48.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,But whenever it fails to finish the task, it will ask the frontier model for help.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.92,0:04:54.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we found that to finish each episode, on average, it costs the frontier model once.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.30,0:05:00.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the next method is called orchestrated policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.98,0:05:06.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Basically, the frontier model will call different skills to finish the task.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:07.27,0:05:13.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And during this process, we found that for each episode, the frontier model is called four times.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.34,0:05:20.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the next one is direct policy that we have just talked about.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:22.18,0:05:24.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,The last method is called agentic policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.16,0:05:35.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's basically for each step, the model can not only itself controls the robot, it can also use tools or math to calculate before it make actions.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.20,0:05:43.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it also, for each step, it can also do, for each step, we also call the frontier model,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.23,0:05:48.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,But we consume more tokens because it also needs to think more and conduct more computations.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:51.76,0:05:58.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,If the thinking time are free, we can ask the model more for results.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:58.96,0:06:02.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we found that all these methods perform pretty well.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:03.90,0:06:12.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Apart from the coding policy only performs 45 % success rate, all the other methods have success rate higher than 60%.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:16.15,0:06:19.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what if we have a real clock?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.05,0:06:27.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,basically the clock runs and the step-by-step policy could fail because of exceeding the time limit.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:29.15,0:06:31.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then the result is totally different.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.31,0:06:41.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,We find that the step-by-step policy fails especially the direct policy and agentic policy only have a success rate of 15 and 20 percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:44.76,0:06:53.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it basically tells us that a slow model is fine as long as it's is combined with a system one.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.07,0:07:03.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, the orchestrated policy uses small neural network skills to deal with the high frequency control.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.03,0:07:14.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,So where exactly do we draw the lines between layers in the orchestrated policy?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:16.35,0:07:19.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,The old school answer can be by functions.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:19.73,0:07:23.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Perception here, planning there, and control at the bottom.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.52,0:07:28.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think today, we want to argue for something else.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:29.12,0:07:33.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,The clock defines the levels, so you draw the lines where the clock changes.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:34.42,0:07:38.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,The Astra is the top layer, System 2, on a clock of seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.64,0:07:42.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Below it, the System 1 is something like a VLA or a WAM.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:42.30,0:07:44.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,They should run at tens of hertz.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.30,0:07:49.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,And System 0 is something like whole body tracker that runs hundreds of hertz.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.90,0:07:59.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,By the way, in this talk, when we talk about system 1, system 2, system 0, we're not talking about thinking, we're talking about time clocks.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:02.89,0:08:06.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then, how do you tell which level something should belong to?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.45,0:08:09.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think about this problem in two steps.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.37,0:08:19.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,First, ask what the layer has to react to, because that tells you how fast the clock has to tick.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.13,0:08:22.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then, the clock tells you the level.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.32,0:08:28.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, system 2 should react to task, state, language, and scene.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:29.08,0:08:32.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it should work from seconds to minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:36.23,0:08:45.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,For system 1, its task is to react to hand-object relative motions at the frequency of tens of hertz.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:47.11,0:08:52.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,For system 0, its job is to keep track of contact and force.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.39,0:08:55.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's why it has to run at hundreds of hertz.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:57.03,0:09:00.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, let's take a slipping cup as an example.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:01.76,0:09:11.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,It involves perception and control all at once, but they all have to change in milliseconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:12.13,0:09:14.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,That is why they are system zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.91,0:09:17.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,A slipping cup is system zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:17.43,0:09:22.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you want to cut it by function, then it's like perception and control.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:25.25,0:09:31.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,I've run into this clock problem before when I was working on self-driving.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:33.71,0:09:41.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,With DriveVLM, we put a vision language model into a car and we found that it has surprising capability in understanding the scene.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:41.09,0:09:47.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, it can understand a school zone, which is slow down to wait for the children to cross the street.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:48.78,0:09:56.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, such vision language models cannot respond immediately to the driving tasks.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:56.34,0:10:03.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we put two clocks together, a slow VLM and a fast driving stack.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.08,0:10:12.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,We put them together onto one car and such systems are already running massively on real production vehicles today.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:13.32,0:10:22.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,So our lesson from self-driving is that we should let the fast layer drive and the slow layer think, each on its own clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:25.47,0:10:29.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,My second lesson is from working on robot parkour.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:30.66,0:10:40.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 2023, our quadruped robot learned parkour from vision to climbing, leaping, crawling, and then we did it on full-size humanoids.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:41.69,0:10:49.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this year, we're able to do whole-body parkour, where the robot uses hands and knees to vault and climb, interacting with the environment.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:50.75,0:10:54.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,All of these are learned controllers at the fast end.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:54.41,0:10:58.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,They read depths by themselves and react in milliseconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:59.03,0:11:01.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the whole system is a system zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:02.91,0:11:08.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,So my lesson from parkour is that the reflexes have to live in the fast layer,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:08.65,0:11:14.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,and the layer needs to do everything by its own, from eyes, perception, to actions.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:18.55,0:11:21.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in real life, things are not that simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:22.03,0:11:24.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,A task often needs more than one layer.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:24.63,0:11:26.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's try a few settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:26.77,0:11:32.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,For industrial assembly line, is it a system 2, 1, or 0?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:33.42,0:11:37.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because it's fixed and repetitive, it only requires system 0.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:38.81,0:11:41.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then how about the next one, logistic sorting?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:42.13,0:11:47.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,The task is prefixed, but every parcel and package is different.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:48.59,0:11:52.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it requires very strong system 1.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:56.15,0:11:58.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,The next setting, restaurant service.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:58.15,0:12:04.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,you need to talk to the customers, place orders, and also hold the place.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:04.96,0:12:06.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you need a bit of everything.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:06.94,0:12:11.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,You need a bit of System 2, a bit of System 1, and a bit of System 0.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:14.07,0:12:16.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,The last one, doing housework.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:17.70,0:12:23.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Housework, the task definition is more complicated than doing restaurant service,
Dialogue: 0,0:12:24.44,0:12:33.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,ranging from that is why it requires very strong System 2 model.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:34.83,0:12:38.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in terms of speed, it can be slow.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:38.63,0:12:42.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we need a slightly weaker system one and a system zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:46.18,0:12:46.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:47.36,0:12:52.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then for this hierarchical systems, we have two more important questions to work on.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:53.68,0:13:01.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first one is, how do we make each layer fast enough by itself so that it catch up with the clock, its own clock?
Dialogue: 0,0:13:02.78,0:13:09.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,The second question is, what should pass between these layers, between these systems, so that the whole system work as a whole.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:13.64,0:13:15.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's talk about the first problem first.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:15.54,0:13:18.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Make each system fast enough, especially system one.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:21.50,0:13:30.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,As we have already talked about, system one should work at tens of hertz because it has to respond to human-object interactions.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:32.27,0:13:35.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, today's system ones are usually quite slow.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:36.51,0:13:39.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,They are not able to catch up with tens of hertz.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:41.08,0:13:41.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why?
Dialogue: 0,0:13:42.43,0:13:44.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,We try to figure out the reason.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:44.09,0:13:55.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,We found that there are three costs and each cost comes from where today's system one models come from if you are building a world action model,
Dialogue: 0,0:13:55.28,0:14:00.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,which is super popular this year and From a video generation model,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:00.57,0:14:08.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,you will know that the video generation model base is actually quite slow Generating a five-second high-quality video
Dialogue: 0,0:14:08.41,0:14:18.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,usually requires two to three minutes to generate them This is not possible to deploy such model on a robot.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:22.66,0:14:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you are building a vision-language action model from a VLM, then the VLM will typically reason in text.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:31.62,0:14:34.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,The reasoning text usually takes like five seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:36.13,0:14:39.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is still not affordable for a robotic control problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:42.48,0:14:47.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the third cost that I found is about action chunking.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:48.34,0:14:56.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Almost all the papers, no matter its VLA, its diffusion policy, its world action models,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:56.36,0:15:00.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,you always decode a whole action chunk before you execute them.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:01.71,0:15:08.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,That means you're always waiting for the whole chunk to be generated before execution, and then you lose a lot of time.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:11.01,0:15:15.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,And for these three problems, we have three works dealing with each of them.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:18.40,0:15:20.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first work is called FastWAM.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:22.24,0:15:25.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,World action models predict future videos along with actions.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:26.16,0:15:27.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's how they learn physics.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:27.82,0:15:30.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And most of them generates that video before they act.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:31.63,0:15:33.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,They also call it IDM mode.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:34.21,0:15:37.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we asked, do we really need to imagine the future at test time?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:39.39,0:15:46.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,FastWAM says that a video model and an action model share attention, and the trick is on these masks.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:47.22,0:15:52.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,If we change the attention mask a little bit during training time, the model needs to generate future videos.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:52.82,0:15:59.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,but at test time it can skip it, then we can speed up the model by more than four times.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:02.06,0:16:11.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,This paper was published early this year, and today we have a more powerful pre-trained version of FastWAM called G0.7.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:12.41,0:16:20.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,The model size goes from 5 billion parameters to 16 billion parameters, with a mixture of transformer architecture.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:20.75,0:16:23.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yet, with optimization, it runs much faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:23.79,0:16:29.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,It runs 60 milliseconds per action on an RTX 5090 GPU.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:34.39,0:16:39.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Next, let's talk about the reasoning in text in vision language action models.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:41.67,0:16:45.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,G0.5 is our robot foundation model that we trained at Galaxea.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:46.05,0:16:53.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it's one VLM model that takes vision and text as input, and write subtasks,
Dialogue: 0,0:16:53.53,0:16:58.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,bounding box, action trajectories, everything in one discrete token stream.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:00.59,0:17:06.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the same checkpoint, we can simply turn the reasoning on and off to test its performance.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:07.01,0:17:14.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Surprisingly, we found that for most of the tasks, 90 % of the tasks, reasoning at inference makes no difference.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:15.61,0:17:18.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Success is the same with and without chain of thought.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:19.01,0:17:22.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if you skip it, the model runs twice as fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:24.17,0:17:27.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where does it help are those long tasks.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:28.21,0:17:34.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, this air fryer task that has five stages, and this is a zero-shot task it has never seen before.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:35.11,0:17:37.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,With reasoning, it can help with the performance.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:41.85,0:17:44.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Next, let's talk about action chunking.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:45.36,0:17:50.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,The cost from action chunking is waiting for the whole action trajectory to be generated.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:51.00,0:17:57.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,This animation is from our recent work called ActionCodec2.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:57.97,0:18:02.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,It shows that the arms are chasing the blocks that keeps moving.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:03.40,0:18:05.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the left is a chunk tokenizer.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:06.14,0:18:12.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,The policy has to decode a whole chunk before it moves, so it only sees the blocks' chunk boundaries.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:13.59,0:18:16.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Looking sooner means decoding a whole new chunk.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:16.93,0:18:21.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the right-hand side is ActionCodec2, a streaming tokenizer.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:21.05,0:18:27.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Every token is a small piece of motion that the robot runs as soon as it arrives.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:28.02,0:18:29.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it sees the block at every step.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:32.22,0:18:36.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Watch the end, the trunk arm misses and the streaming arm gets it.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.00,0:18:43.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's what we mean by streaming action tokenizer and we can use it to build a streaming VLA.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:53.31,0:18:58.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay, the second part I want to talk about decide what passes between different systems.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:01.06,0:19:07.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,With these three clocks, we have two boundaries, and each goes both ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:08.46,0:19:11.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,The intent goes from system two to system one.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:11.84,0:19:16.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can pass language, goals, programs, and action macro intents.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:16.98,0:19:20.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,From system one to system zero, we can pass motion intents.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:24.21,0:19:31.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the other way around, from system zero to system one, we can feedback with contacts, force, and tracking states.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:31.61,0:19:40.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,and from system one to system two, we can also feedback progress, confidence, and some state signals.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:40.99,0:19:47.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, most of today's work is on the way down, and the way up is still heavily missing.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:49.85,0:19:51.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's first talk about the way down.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:53.31,0:19:55.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have this work called whole body WAN.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:56.21,0:19:59.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a world action model built for humanoid robots.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:00.69,0:20:07.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,For a system one, it outputs the body and hand poses with future action trajectories.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:08.15,0:20:10.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we send it to system zero for it to track.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:12.28,0:20:13.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's quite straightforward, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:14.16,0:20:17.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can even make the system one more complicated.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:17.58,0:20:19.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,This work is called OMG.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:19.76,0:20:23.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a versatile one motion generator with many inputs.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:24.78,0:20:29.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,As a system two, you can send text motions, motions in text.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:29.86,0:20:31.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can send motions in music.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:31.46,0:20:34.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can send motions in human poses to system one.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:34.80,0:20:42.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,and the system one will turn them into an executable trajectory and finally the system zero will tracks it.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:47.71,0:20:51.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Although we have rich interface between system two and system one,
Dialogue: 0,0:20:52.19,0:20:56.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,the system between system one and system zero we still only have motions going down.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:59.93,0:21:00.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is motion enough?
Dialogue: 0,0:21:00.97,0:21:01.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course not.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:02.43,0:21:07.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,First notice that the tracker follows the motion without knowing what's it for and I
Dialogue: 0,0:21:07.58,0:21:12.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,don't think motion is not enough because when we talk about interaction and a contact.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:13.17,0:21:17.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, when you wipe a table, it's mostly forced and very little motion.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:18.45,0:21:24.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,When you sit down, the motion looks the same whether or not the chair takes your weight on takes your weight.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:25.91,0:21:31.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the people are starting to go beyond motion from joint targets to body motion to contacts.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:31.65,0:21:32.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now to force.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:36.76,0:21:42.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first step beyond motion is keep the contact when you move motion onto a new body.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:43.88,0:21:49.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Omni-Retarget and our Topo-Retarget, that's published this time at CoRL,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:49.50,0:21:55.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,all keeps trying to preserve the contact points and geometry between the robot and the object.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:00.04,0:22:03.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,But honestly, both of these only keep the contact geometry.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:03.76,0:22:06.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,Neither of them knows anything about force yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:07.72,0:22:11.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And very recently, I noticed a work from UC Berkeley called Control Force.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:14.17,0:22:22.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,It gives the tracker a reference motion plus a force command for each hand and a small planner on top picks the force from what it sees.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:23.39,0:22:27.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here's a robot that pushes a heavy box that is 20 kilograms inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:28.48,0:22:32.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the left, Sonic only tracks the motion and can't move it.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:32.78,0:22:39.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the right their model pushes it and notice who picks the force.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:39.94,0:22:49.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a small planner that is system one that watches the object close to the body, and it's not a slow model from far above.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:55.17,0:22:57.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And next, let's look at some failure cases.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:57.99,0:22:59.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where does the system fails?
Dialogue: 0,0:23:00.33,0:23:03.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,It often fails at the hand-offs between the systems.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:04.33,0:23:11.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,For example, most of the time, the lower level system acts by itself, but it does not know it has already failed.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:11.97,0:23:18.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the higher level system is not quick enough to notice that the task has already failed.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:20.07,0:23:22.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is the hand-off issue.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:25.75,0:23:30.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,And on the right, a Sonic model lifting a two-kilogram box.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:30.54,0:23:36.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,It follows the upper level motion command, but the hands slide off.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:36.50,0:23:40.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,It has no way to tell anyone that the box never came up.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:41.87,0:23:46.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,So every one of these failures sit on a boundary between the clocks.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:47.21,0:23:51.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today, the feedback across that boundary is simply missing.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:52.22,0:23:57.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,and I argue that we urgently need this feedback mechanism.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:02.21,0:24:05.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let me end with where I think the future is going for.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:06.23,0:24:10.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,A chatbot can wait for the model, it's fine, but a robot cannot.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:10.81,0:24:13.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,So my view is that the future goes two ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:14.11,0:24:23.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first way is going with layers, each on its own clock, but the layer above shouldn't try to give the exact contact of forces.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:24.02,0:24:27.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's too slow to get them right.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:27.68,0:24:33.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,It sends intent downwards and the lower level systems sends feedback upwards.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:35.05,0:24:43.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the hard part for this kind of design is that the lower level system must also sense correct and send feedback on its own.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:48.19,0:24:53.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the second way is the one model way that everybody who work on deep learning likes.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:53.43,0:24:57.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's that we want to build a unified one model that share weights.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:57.51,0:25:02.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,but each system or each clock has its own expert.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:03.23,0:25:04.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,They're running at different clocks.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:05.76,0:25:13.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,The good part about this design is that we have a unified architecture with shared contacts, maybe with transformers we can share their KV caches.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:13.90,0:25:15.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's great, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:15.90,0:25:20.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then at inference time, they run at different clocks with flexibility.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:21.49,0:25:29.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the hard part for this kind of model system design is how do we train system 0, system 1, and system 2 all together.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:30.67,0:25:35.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Training them all together have a lot of difficulties because today system 1 and system 2
Dialogue: 0,0:25:35.41,0:25:41.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,are mostly trained from with behavior cloning while system 0 is trained in simulation with RL.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:41.91,0:25:47.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do we combine these two training mechanisms into one environment and train them together?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.03,0:25:53.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think either way the clock has to be part of the model design.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:56.55,0:26:01.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,So to wrap up, the world doesn't wait for the model in robotics.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:01.81,0:26:04.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,What a system must react to set its clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:06.31,0:26:12.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we need tens of hertz for arms and hands and milliseconds for contact and force.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:13.91,0:26:18.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we need to pick the model that fit the clock, not the other way around.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:19.12,0:26:25.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Between systems, we need to send intents down and let each of them close its own loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:26.66,0:26:32.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the lower levels should send feedbacks or failures alert to the upper systems.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:33.23,0:26:36.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,So don't just build one fast model or one small model.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:36.97,0:26:38.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,You need to build a whole system.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:39.43,0:26:45.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,At the end of the day, we need to design a multi-clock system and work on what passes between these clocks.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:46.98,0:26:55.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,And my one last request to the community all of us is that later when you build a simulation
Dialogue: 0,0:26:55.15,0:27:04.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,benchmark for robotics also report a success rate before the robots deadline and also the inference time that is required.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:06.79,0:27:09.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay that's everything that I want to share today.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:09.93,0:27:18.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,All of my work is done with my amazing students at Tsinghua MARS Lab, and some of them are here today at CoRL, so please come and talk to us.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:18.59,0:27:19.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.
